 ],
 "literal": "Who afflicted you, O sea? / Like the small-headed white flock of the Pūḻiyar spread out, / in the beautiful seaside grove's great shore of fish-eating herons, / where the waves beat the white-flowered screwpine, / even in the dark midnight, your voice is heard.",
 "translation": "Who has caused you distress, O sea? Even in the dead of night, your voice is heard crying out on this great shore with its beautiful seaside groves, where fish-eating herons gather like the spread-out flocks of small-headed white sheep belonging to the Pūḻiyar, and where waves beat against the white-flowered screwpine trees.",
 "modern": "கடலே! பூழியர்களுடைய சிறிய தலைகளைக் கொண்ட வெள்ளாட்டு மந்தைகள் பரவி இருப்பது போல, மீன்களை உண்ணும் நாரைகள் பரவிக் கிடக்கின்ற, அழகிய கடற்கரைச் சோலையையுடைய பெரிய துறையில், வெண்மையான மலர்களைக் கொண்ட தாழை மரங்களை அலைகள் மோதும் நள்ளிரவிலும் உன்னுடைய குரல் கேட்கிறதே! உனக்குத் துன்பம் செய்தவர் யார்?",
 "explanation": "The heroine, suffering from the pain of separation from her lover, addresses the roaring sea. She compares her own sleeplessness and constant crying to the endless sound of the waves. She asks the sea who has caused it such distress that it keeps wailing even in the dead of night, just as she does. The image of the white herons spread across the shore like the white sheep of the Pūḻi country highlights the vast, lonely expanse of the neytal (coastal) landscape. By projecting her own grief onto the sea, she expresses the depth of her sorrow.",
